.TH PROLEWATCH 1 "September 2026" "Prolewatch" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
prolewatch \- contain and inspect AUR builds driven by yay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prolewatch
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prolewatch connects to yay through its Lua configuration. It inspects the AUR
recipe, fetched sources, and built package. Package-controlled build code runs
in a Bubblewrap sandbox with a temporary home, private namespaces, brokered
network access, and systemd resource limits.
.PP
Before pacman receives the package, Prolewatch displays known privileged
integration such as install scriptlets, pacman hooks, and udev rules.
.SH FIRST USE
After installing the package, run:
.PP
.EX
prolewatch setup
.EE
.PP
Setup checks the installed files, configuration, sandbox, user manager,
subordinate IDs, and supported yay integration. It installs the yay hook only
after the required checks pass.
.PP
Subsequent AUR transactions use the normal yay command:
.PP
.EX
yay -S package
.EE
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Run the required health checks and activate the yay integration for the current
user.
.TP
.B doctor
Check the installation, configuration, containment, and active review provider.
Use
.B --no-probe
to skip a live provider request or
.B --json
for machine-readable output.
.TP
.B inspect " REPORT_ID"
Open every stored finding with source context. Use
.B --latest
instead of a report ID to inspect the newest report.
.TP
.B report " REPORT_ID"
Print a stored report. The
.B --latest
option selects the newest report.
.TP
.B approve " REPORT_ID"
Create a one-time approval for an eligible report. Structural failures remain
ineligible.
.TP
.B config-check
Validate the configuration. Use
.BI --path " FILE"
to check another file.
.TP
.B llm-benchmark
Benchmark the configured review provider. Provider setup and benchmark options
are documented in the installed AI review guide.
.TP
.B install-hook
Install only the yay hook. Normal installations should use
.B setup
so the complete preflight runs first.
.TP
.B uninstall-hook
Remove Prolewatch's managed block from yay's configuration. Unrelated yay
configuration and backups remain in place.
.TP
.B scan
Run a recipe, source, or artifact scan. The yay hook and wrapper commands supply
the required transaction context during normal use.
.TP
.B security-scenarios
Run the installed deterministic security scenario corpus.
.TP
.B version
Print the installed version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/prolewatch/config.yaml
System policy and provider configuration. Pacman preserves local edits during
upgrades.
.TP
.I ~/.config/yay/init.lua
Contains a clearly marked block that loads the Prolewatch integration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/yay/prolewatch.lua
The managed yay hook module.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/prolewatch/
Reports, build logs, approvals, quarantine data, and provider attestations.
The XDG state directory is used when configured.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully or policy allowed the scanned input.
.TP
.B 10
Policy blocked the input.
.TP
.B 20
The command line or configuration is invalid.
.TP
.B 21
Inspection failed.
.TP
.B 22
Required review or health checks are unavailable.
.TP
.B 23
Persistent state or integration failed.
.TP
.B 24
Contained execution failed.
.TP
.B 25
Artifact validation failed.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
The sandbox shares the host kernel. The final package installation remains a
sudo pacman transaction. Prolewatch reviews builds and package integration; it
does not monitor installed programs at runtime.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR prolewatch-config (5),
.BR yay (8),
.BR makepkg (8),
.BR pacman (8)
